# ============================================================================
# CITATION.cff — how to cite EML Stats & Graphs
#
# Ian Howell — Embodied Music Lab — GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# Citation File Format 1.2.0. GitHub reads this file AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT
# and nowhere else, and puts a "Cite this repository" button on the front
# page from it. Zenodo reads it when it archives a published release, which
# is how a release becomes a DOI that a journal will accept.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS NOW. Publishing a release with a Zenodo integration
# switched on mints a DOI for that release automatically, and the PraatGen
# paper can then cite the archived software directly instead of waiting for
# a journal to publish something to cite. .github/workflows/release.yml
# publishes the release; ENABLING THE ZENODO INTEGRATION IS A BROWSER STEP
# IAN TAKES HIMSELF (Zenodo -> GitHub -> toggle this repository on) and is
# deliberately not automated here: it authorises an outside service against
# the account, which is not a thing a workflow file should be able to do.
# The toggle must be on BEFORE the release is published — Zenodo acts on the
# release event and does not go back for releases made earlier.
#
# WHAT ZENODO ARCHIVES is the repository snapshot for the tag — git's own
# source archive — not the release assets. So this file must be in the
# repository at the tag, and must NOT be excluded from source archives by
# `.gitattributes` `export-ignore`. If the export-ignore list ever grows to
# name it, the DOI landing page loses its author and title metadata.
#
# THREE FIELDS ARE LEFT FOR IAN, and are marked FOR IAN below. They are left
# blank rather than guessed because each of them is a claim that would be
# wrong if invented: an ORCID belongs to a person and cannot be inferred; a
# DOI does not exist until Zenodo mints one; and a version number and its
# release date are the author's decision about what the tag means. NOTHING
# HERE IS A PLACEHOLDER PRETENDING TO BE A VALUE — the lines are commented
# out, so the file validates as it stands and says only what is true.
#
# THIS FILE DOES NOT TRAVEL INSIDE THE RELEASE ZIP, and that is correct
# rather than an oversight. plugin/dev/tools/build-release.py stages plugin/
# alone; everything beside plugin/ is outside the artefact by the shape of
# the tree, exactly as RELEASE_EXCLUDE.tsv's header describes for itself. A
# second copy inside plugin/ would be a second statement of the version and
# the DOI that could disagree with this one, and the version in a download
# is the version most likely to go stale. Cite the repository or the DOI,
# not the copy in your Praat preferences folder.
#
# Checked with cffconvert 2.0.0 (`cffconvert --validate`) and parsed with
# PyYAML; both were watched failing on a deliberately broken copy.
# ============================================================================
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
title: "EML Stats & Graphs"
abstract: >-
  A Praat plugin for statistical analysis and publication-quality graphing.
  It runs descriptive statistics, t-tests, correlations, ANOVA, regression
  and nonparametric tests inside Praat, without exporting to R or Python,
  and draws figures with auto-scaling typography and accessible colour
  palettes. It includes a statistics wizard that proposes an appropriate
  test for the data at hand, a demonstration window, and a scripting API for
  use from a user's own Praat scripts.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Howell
    given-names: Ian
    affiliation: "Embodied Music Lab"
    # FOR IAN — your ORCID, if you would like it on the citation and the
    # Zenodo record. Uncomment and replace the digits with your own iD.
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/embodied-music-lab/EMLPraatTools"
url: "https://www.embodiedmusiclab.com"
keywords:
  - Praat
  - voice science
  - phonetics
  - speech analysis
  - statistics
  - data visualization
  - research software
# FOR IAN — the released version and the date it was released. These are
# claims about a specific release, so they are yours to set at the moment
# you tag one; they should match the tag that release.yml published.
# version: "1.0.0"
# date-released: "2026-08-17"
#
# FOR IAN — the DOI, after Zenodo has minted one. Zenodo issues two: a
# version DOI for that single release and a CONCEPT DOI that always resolves
# to the newest version. The concept DOI is the one to put here if this file
# is to age well; the version DOI belongs in a paper that cites the exact
# release it used.
# identifiers:
#   - type: doi
#     value: "10.5281/zenodo.0000000"
#     description: "Concept DOI — always resolves to the latest release"
